index 123559c..caa54c9 100644
--- a/apps/issue-pipeline/README.md
+++ b/apps/issue-pipeline/README.md
@@ -110,6 +110,28 @@ Issue/branch binding is persisted at first claim. A changed branch on resume is
 
 Parallel jobs must use distinct issues/branches. Never dispatch the same issue/branch twice concurrently, even from the same lane. The caller serializes integration/merge turns (one at a time); existing claim/lease/head/merge-queue guards still apply. `--merge-queue N,...` carries a caller-supplied order, not permission to run concurrent turns. Keep each issue's assignment and claimant stable across restarts. Job checkouts are isolated under `work/<slug>/jobs/`; shutdown saves owned recovery patches and cleans only owned process groups. A signal exits 128 + signal, saving the last checkpoint with no lease.
 
+## Instance fencing and integration turns — implementation draft (#116)
+
+**This branch is incomplete and is not an install candidate.** The protocol seam has deterministic tests, but existing CLI/job fixtures still assume legacy claims and need migration with their delivery and recovery assertions intact. See [preserved development status](preserved/issue-116.md). Do not treat these draft semantics as a validated installed runtime.
+
+The draft separates the stable caller `--lane` from a fresh UUID for each invocation, independent of caller-supplied job IDs. Only a confirmed execution instance may launch a writer or cross a controlled mutation boundary. Sequential stages acquire anew after the predecessor saved its authoritative state, completed recovery-safe teardown and verifiably relinquished ownership. Stage mismatch saves preserved work without launching a model. A lease timestamp, matching lane or stale in-memory state is never execution permission.
+
+Authenticated append-only GitHub comments represent requests, confirmations, checkpoints and releases. Immutable create-only refs under `pipeline-coordination/` arbitrate each repository/base-scoped ownership epoch; those refs are coordination metadata, not source delivery. Releases refer to one exact request/instance and open a new epoch without deleting the predecessor's ref. Request/comment order is retained across restarts; it is not an autonomous scheduling or fairness policy. A missing/changed historical lock, malformed record, foreign author/editor or conflicting instance holds for reconciliation rather than selecting older authority. Comment provenance still requires merge authorship and same-read editor verification.
+
+Compatibility in the draft:
+
+- Genuinely new issues retain first-claim checks against prior GitHub/local work.
+- V2 saved state with a strict `execution` receipt (`protocol: 1`, request comment ID and instance UUID) requires its authenticated complete coordination history and immutable refs. Active ownership is not inherited by a new invocation; only verified release permits sequential reacquisition.
+- V2 state without the receipt, old live/expired lane claims, unknown/conflicting write outcomes, malformed receipts and missing history require explicit reconciliation. V1 remains display-only. No automatic legacy adoption is supplied.
+- A write intent is preserved locally as `coordination-uncertain.json` before the attempt. Exact authenticated read-back can conclusively establish an outcome despite a lost response. Otherwise the intent remains and the write is not automatically repeated, including on an explicit restart. Do not delete it to obtain permission.
+- Dirty-work patches, unresolved indexes, retained checkouts and required code/wiki recovery remain recovery requirements, not disposable coordination caches. A recovery-blocked executor does not relinquish merely because its heartbeat is old. Refused work is preserved locally, never pushed for safekeeping.
+
+`integrationTurn` is an explicit protocol primitive only: **the default runtime does not invoke it**. Its repository/base scope is shared across issues. Future assignment-mode composition must route caller-dispatched merge jobs and assignment candidates through that same guard, check both issue-instance and turn ownership freshly at controlled integration boundaries, and retain merge-queue order as an additional restriction. Caller dispatch, a supplied queue or a local lock cannot independently grant a turn. Existing branch/head/CI/approval/trusted-input/wiki/security and merged-result controls remain restrictions; no exclusion of outside GitHub actors is claimed.
+
+### Drained operator-owned cutover
+
+Older runtimes do not enforce the draft instance or integration guards. Mixed old/new executors, waiting for lease expiry and automatically adopting legacy state are **not** safe cutover strategies. Once the exact source and tests are complete and reviewed, the operator must stop dispatch, drain every old executor, preserve authoritative GitHub state/comments/refs, uncertain-write evidence and all required local recovery artifacts, and reconcile incompatible or unresolved ownership explicitly before a coordinated replacement. Do not clear receipts, order, merged/security evidence or recovery files to make work look new. Installation, worker activation, credentials and deployment remain operator-owned and are not authorized by source delivery. This change adds no discovery, polling, handoff or activation option.
+
 ## Write authority and refused-work recovery
 
 Every credentialed code push, including stop-time salvage and synchronization, checks fresh merge-authored, editor-verified saved authority and actual PR status. Missing or malformed critical state, a saved code merge, a security rejection or a confirmed live merge grants no code write. Sync checks authority before resetting or rebasing local work, as well as before its push. A diagnostic comment quoting a state marker is not a state comment; the latest genuine merge-authored, editor-verified state remains authoritative, including when it is malformed and must be recovered rather than ignored. Claims validate existing state before posting or labeling; only a genuine first claim initializes absent state, and any prior pipeline comment, local work, remote issue branch, private wiki ref or PR history without saved authority requires reconciliation. Legacy v1 state is display-only and claims refuse it with `legacy pipeline state needs explicit reconciliation`; there is no automatic adoption tool. Checkpoints and releases require already-established v2 authority. Reauthoring also validates the fresh mutation state, rather than saving tolerant display defaults as new permission.
